					],
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "ek34vQYkYoyL7oO7I",
					"description": "Jet Pack (Crisis Pattern)",
					"local_notes": "Flight, in short bursts: Air Move 12, 8 seconds of thrust per fuel load (each turn of thrust uses 1). It can hover briefly (a turn spent hovering costs one turn of thrust) but cannot sustain flight: the pilot must be landed when the fuel is gone. A full-burn hop carries the suit about 60 yards up and 90 across. Refuel about 10 minutes. Exhaust wash 1d burn to anyone within 2 yards of a landing.\n\nLore: The Crisis suit's jet pack is its trademark: framework, jet pack for short flight bursts.\n\nDesign: Scaled from the Astartes jump pack (Air Move 15, 6 s, 60 lb) and the Sororitas Seraphim pack: a heavier suit (about 4x the Sororitas's) gets Air Move 12 and 8 s for a 55 lb pack; hovering is allowed briefly where the Astartes pack forbids it.",
					"tech_level": "11",
					"legality_class": "0",
					"base_value": "18000",
					"base_weight": "55 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "eZp4yKNy-JwEl6PGM",
					"description": "Hardpoint Mounts (Crisis Pattern, 3)",
					"local_notes": "Three hardpoints: each carries one weapon or one support system (for example two weapons and a shield generator). Weapons and systems are separate items and are not included; the suit is not pre-loaded.\n\nLore: Framework: three hardpoints for weapon and support systems.\n\nDesign: The mounting, feed and power hardware for three fittings, priced apart from what they carry.",
					"tech_level": "11",
					"legality_class": "0",
					"base_value": "6000",
					"base_weight": "30 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "edhXC2M06izniv8nX",
					"description": "Cockpit Interface, Sensors and Life Support (Crisis Pattern)",
					"local_notes": "Sealed cockpit, comm-link, display, targeting sensors and air recycling (about a day). +1 to Observation.\n\nLore: Battlesuit pilots fight by instruments; the suit's interface is why a T'au can fight in it.\n\nDesign: Flat +1, as the Stealth suit. Weight includes sensors and air supply (14 lb + 12 lb); flavour subsystem.",
					"tech_level": "11",
					"legality_class": "0",
					"base_value": "9000",
					"base_weight": "26 lb",
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "skill_bonus",
							"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Observation"
							},
							"amount": 1
						}
					],
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "EXQItazC40YwDOVQS",
			"description": "XV88 Broadside Battlesuit",
			"local_notes": "Weak Points 3: when a hit on this armour fails to penetrate, roll 3d; on 3 or less it found a gap and DR is halved against it.\n\nBattlesuit rules. Sealed pilot: the suit is airtight against hostile atmospheres; extended vacuum operation is the GM's call. It needs trained handling: an untrained wearer is at -4 to all DX rolls and cannot use linked fire or the support systems. Linked fire (house rule, since GURPS lets one Attack maneuver fire one weapon): the pilot may fire all mounted weapons in one Attack maneuver at one target, rolling separately for each at -1 per weapon beyond the first that turn. A Multi-tracker removes that penalty and a Target Lock lets weapons fire at different targets (see those items). Size: about 3 m, SM +1. Heavy and slow: Basic Move -1 while worn (a T'au's Move 5 becomes 4). No jet pack. Two weapon hardpoints (one heavy) and one support hardpoint. GCS adds a container's children to its own weight and cost: the shell alone is 255 lb and $65,000; with its three children (306 lb, $76,000) the assembled suit is 561 lb and $141,000. Servo ST lives on the Servo-Motor child only, so it is counted once.\n\nLore: The T'au heavy-weapons suit: Codex: T'au Empire background describes the XV88 Broadside as a heavily armoured, slow platform for the heavy rail rifle and secondary missile weapons, the T'au long-range anti-armour line, positioned behind the front and fed by markerlight coordinates. The framework fixes DR, Weak Points, weight class and lack of a jet pack; the rest is design.\n\nDesign: Anchor (framework): DR 170, Weak Points 3; heavy and slow, no jet pack. Torso 170 and WP 3 held exactly; limbs 152 (the same 0.89 ratio to torso as the Crisis and Astartes limbs), neck 118, helmet 170, face 132, eye lens 55 (between Crisis 45/110 and Terminator 60/200, about 32% of torso), hands 105, feet 132. Exact odds against the torso (DR 170, WP 3): lasgun 7d(2) 0.0%; autogun 7d 0.0%; boltgun or pulse rifle 6dx2(2) 0.0% (0.2% with WP 3); heavy bolter 7dx2(2) 0.0% (0.3% with WP 3); plasma gun 6dx4(3) 93.9%; autocannon 6dx3(3) 72.1% (72.2% with WP 3); krak missile 6dx6(3) 99.8%; lascannon 8dx8(5) 100.0%; meltagun, short range 8dx6(10) 100.0%; Astartes power sword at ST 30, 8d+3(10) 99.8%. So it is Terminator-adjacent, a little under: bolters and pulse rifles are stopped (0.0%, 0.1% with WP), a heavy bolter 0.0% (0.3% with WP), an autocannon 72.1%, a plasma gun 93.9% (Terminator plate 85.5%), a krak missile 99.8%, a lascannon and meltagun always. Eye lens (DR 55): lasgun 25.7%, boltgun 96.4%, plasma 100.0%. Weight: shell 255 lb, 1.5 lb per DR point, children 306 lb. Servo Lifting +26 / Striking +13: a pilot of ST 11 is a 37-ST lifter, meeting the 90 lb heavy rail rifle's Min ST 30M. Cost: shell $65,000, servos $60,000, hardpoints $6,000, cockpit $10,000: $141,000 assembled. Basic Move -1, as Gravis armour: a heavy, jetless frame.",
