		},
		{
			"id": "ecFUmYJs8-H63hpp_",
			"description": "Shield Generator",
			"local_notes": "Regenerating shield (framework, Personal force fields): 120 SP, 3 seconds without damage before it recharges, then 20 SP per second, covering all attacks. Damage from an attack the field covers comes off SP first; armour divisors do not apply to SP. If an attack does more damage than the SP remaining, the field drops to 0 and the excess passes to worn armour with the attack's full armour divisor. A bolt stopped by the field detonates on it: its follow-up damage also comes off SP. A field knocked to 0 needs double its delay (6 seconds) before recharging. Track current SP with the Uses counter (max 120). Fitted to a battlesuit support hardpoint.\n\nLore: A staple support system of Crisis and Broadside battlesuits: Codex: T'au Empire background shows shield generators as a common fitting on battlesuits and Commanders, projecting a protective energy field around the suit.\n\nDesign: Anchor (framework): 'regenerating shield, iron-halo class (120 SP, 3 s, 20/s)'. Held exactly. The Imperial iron halo's own table row is 120 SP, delay 3 s, 20 SP/s, covering all; so a T'au shield generator is an iron halo in every number: a plasma gun hit (average 84) takes 84 of 120 SP; a bolter hit (42) plus its follow-up takes about 63 SP, so two bolts drop it; a lasgun hit averages 24.5, so five; a lascannon hit (average 224) breaks it and drives about 100 into the armour with divisor (5). Weight 20 lb and cost $30,000: a heavy support system sized to fit a battlesuit hardpoint (design numbers).",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit",
				"Force Field",
				"Energy Shield"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "30000",
			"base_weight": "20 lb",
			"uses": 120,
			"max_uses": 120,
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "egRqgh4KMhlQZr4oy",
			"description": "Multi-tracker",
			"local_notes": "Fire-control link for a battlesuit's weapons: the -1 per extra weapon for linked fire (see the battlesuit notes) is removed, so a pilot can fire all mounted weapons in one Attack maneuver at one target with no penalty. Occupies one support hardpoint. It does not let weapons fire at different targets: that is Target Lock.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background lists a multi-tracker among battlesuit support systems; its exact effect varies between editions of the rules and I do not hold a firm version, so this is a modelled effect.\n\nDesign: No anchor. The house linked-fire rule sets a -1 per weapon beyond the first when several weapons fire in one maneuver, since GURPS otherwise allows one weapon per Attack maneuver. The multi-tracker's role, as a system that improves how a suit's fire is managed, is honestly modelled as removing that penalty (worth at most -2 on a three-weapon suit). Weight 8 lb, cost $6,000.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "6000",
			"base_weight": "8 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eIpCJJmCXT_dSWgRe",
			"description": "Target Lock",
			"local_notes": "Independent weapon aiming: when linked-fire weapons are fired in one Attack maneuver, each may be assigned to a different target. Each additional target beyond the first costs -2 to that weapon's roll (this does not stack with the -1 per extra weapon, which a Multi-tracker removes). Occupies one support hardpoint.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background lists target lock among battlesuit support systems; the wording of its effect changes between editions, so the numbers here are the modeller's, not the Codex's.\n\nDesign: No anchor. Each extra target costs -2 to its weapon's roll: a design number, kept simple. Weight 10 lb, cost $8,000.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "8000",
			"base_weight": "10 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eQ5Rg53AfwB-647st",
			"description": "Drone Controller",
			"local_notes": "A command link for up to two drones: the pilot can order them (attack, guard, follow, spot, shield) as a free action on the pilot's turn; drones act on the pilot's initiative and use their own stats (a template matter). Radio range about one mile. Without a controller a battlesuit cannot direct drones. Occupies one support hardpoint.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background lists a drone controller among the Crisis suit's supports; drones (gun, marker, shield) are the T'au's cheap, expendable auxiliaries.\n\nDesign: Framework: drones are small flying AI units built as templates, like the servitor. The controller is the link. TL 11^ because drone AI is ^ in the framework. Two drones is the standard load; weight 12 lb, cost $9,000.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit"
			],
			"tech_level": "11^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "9000",
			"base_weight": "12 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eXq0YZO-VfIZ-tKhV",
			"description": "Early Warning Override",
			"local_notes": "Proximity sensors and a reflex link: +3 to Per rolls to notice enemies approaching or arriving within 20 yards, and once per fight the pilot may interrupt to fire one hardpoint weapon (no penalty) at the first hostile that comes within 12 yards before it acts. Occupies one support hardpoint.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background lists an early warning override among battlesuit support systems; as I recall, the tabletop version is an out-of-turn shot at arriving enemies, which is what I have modelled.\n\nDesign: No anchor. The tabletop effect is a reaction fire at an enemy that arrives; +3 to notice and a once-per-fight interrupt is the minimum honest GURPS rendering. Weight 8 lb, cost $5,000.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "5000",
			"base_weight": "8 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "euwssqHN70K4Tp0OJ",
			"description": "Velocity Tracker",
			"local_notes": "Tracking sensors for fast targets: +2 to hit with mounted weapons against flyers and against targets whose speed penalty is -5 or worse. It does nothing against slow or stationary targets. Occupies one support hardpoint.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background lists a velocity tracker among battlesuit support systems, for shooting fast aircraft and skimmers.\n\nDesign: No anchor. A flat +2 against the hardest targets in GURPS speed/range terms (speed penalty -5 is a fast vehicle or aircraft at range). Weight 8 lb, cost $4,000.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "4000",
			"base_weight": "8 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e23bkv-2y512IgsmF",
			"description": "Advanced Targeting System",
			"local_notes": "A targeting computer: +1 to hit with all mounted weapons (in addition to the weapon's own Acc). Occupies one support hardpoint.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background lists an advanced targeting system among battlesuit supports; it improves the accuracy of fire. Its exact rule differs between editions, so +1 is the modeller's choice.\n\nDesign: No anchor. A +1 to hit is the smallest meaningful GURPS bonus, an honest rendering of an improved-accuracy system. Weight 10 lb, cost $7,000.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Battlesuit"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "7000",
			"base_weight": "10 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "ePaVbR6TKpHB0QpXi",
			"description": "Ethereal Hover Drone",
			"local_notes": "Grav platform: Flight (hover, grav-lift) at Air Move 8, altitude to 20 yards, can carry an Ethereal (and up to 250 lb in all) standing on it, and can hover indefinitely. No jet-pack fuel limit: it is grav-lift. The platform is a small vehicle: HP 30, DR 25, SM 0. It follows the Ethereal's orders as a drone (see the drone templates).\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background shows Ethereals attended by hover drones and moving on grav platforms; I hold the exact form (a platform the Ethereal stands on, versus a drone that attends) with low confidence, so this entry is a flight platform with drone control.\n\nDesign: The framework asks for a flight platform; T'au grav systems are the ^ TL exception. Air Move 8 (about 16 mph, slow: a dignitary's progress rather than a jet), 20-yard altitude, 250 lb load, 60 lb. HP 30 and DR 25 make it a small vehicle: a lasgun hit (7d(2)) penetrates it 99.7% of the time. All numbers are design choices; no source gives them.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"T'au",
				"Ethereal"
			],
			"tech_level": "11^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "20000",
			"base_weight": "60 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		}
	]
}
